Server-Side Includes (SSI) Injection

Server-Side Includes (SSI) Injection is a vulnerability where user-controlled input is interpreted as SSI directives by the web server. 
If SSI is enabled and input is not properly validated or encoded, an attacker may be able to read files, access server information, or execute commands depending on the server configuration.

SSI is supported by web servers such as Apache and IIS to dynamically generate web pages.

Common SSI Directives:
Syntax: <!--#directive parameter=value -->

1)  Include a File
<!--#include file="header.html" -->

2)  Execute a Program (if enabled)
<!--#exec cmd="ls-la" -->

By using this payload which contains the ip address and listener port of the attacker machine and attacker starts a listener to get a reverse shell

look for files which end with .stm, .shtm, .shtml

Impact:
Read sensitive server files.
View server configuration information.
Potentially achieve Remote Code Execution

mitigation:

disable SSI where it is not required. 
If SSI is needed, validate and encode user input, 
disable dangerous directives such as exec,
apply the principle of least privilege, 
restrict file permissions, and keep the web server updated.

