Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI):

Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) is a vulnerability that occurs when an application embeds untrusted user input into a server-side template, 
allowing the template engine to interpret that input as template code instead of plain text.

If the application renders user input without proper validation or escaping, an attacker may be able to manipulate the template logic. 
Depending on the template engine and application configuration, this can result in sensitive data disclosure or even Remote Code Execution (RCE).

Common template engines include:

Language	Template Engine
Python --> 	Jinja2, Mako
PHP	--> Twig, Smarty
Java --> Thymeleaf, FreeMarker

Example:

Template:
<h1>Welcome {{ username }}</h1>
If: username = Pradeep
The server renders:
<h1>Welcome Pradeep</h1>

Impact

Read sensitive application data.
Access server configuration information.
Enumerate application objects.
Remote Code Execution (RCE).

Prevent SSTI:

1. Never Treat User Input as Template Code
The best mitigation is to ensure user input is treated as data, not as part of the template.
2. Use Safe Template Rendering
Pass user input as template variables rather than dynamically constructing templates from user-controlled input.
3. Input Validation
6. Run Applications with Least Privilege
7. Keep Template Engines Updated

